   18        'Explain the sacred mathematics behind Kolam patterns',

   23  
   24: export interface CultureQA {
   25    keywords: string[];
   26    response: string;
   27:   source: {
   28      org: string;

   31    };
   32:   followUps: string[];
   33  }
   34  
   35: export const curatedCulturalAnswers: CultureQA[] = [
   36    {
   37:     keywords: ['thanjavur', 'brihadisvara', 'big temple', 'shadow', 'chola temple'],
   38:     response: 'The legendary Brihadisvara Temple in Thanjavur (consecrated in 1010 CE by Raja Raja Chola I) is an architectural miracle. While a popular myth states that its 216-foot vimana "casts no shadow", rigorous architectural surveys by the Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) show that its geometric proportions create an illusion: the vimana’s shadow falls onto its own tiered base (Upapeedha) rather than spreading wide onto the surrounding compound during noon! Furthermore, the monolithic granite capstone (Kumbam) weighs 80 tonnes, hoisted up a 6-km inclined earthen ramp.',
   39:     source: {
   40:       org: 'Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) & UNESCO',
   41        citation: 'World Heritage Dossier #250bis (Great Living Chola Temples)',
   42:       category: 'Temple Architecture & Engineering'
   43      },
   44:     followUps: [
   45:       'What are the musical pillars in South Indian temples?',
   46:       'How were Chola bronze sculptures cast?',
   47:       'Tell me about Kanchipuram silk weaving.'
   48      ]

   50    {
   51:     keywords: ['indigo', 'ajrakh', 'dye', 'natural', 'kutch', 'block print'],
   52:     response: 'Ajrakh printing is a 16-step artisanal alchemy practiced by the Khatri community of Kutch, Gujarat and Sindh. Natural indigo is obtained through microbial fermentation of *Indigofera tinctoria* leaves with lime, jaggery, and dates in large earthen vats until it turns amber-green with a sweet coppery sheen. When the dipped fabric meets air, oxygen instantly turns it into that hypnotic royal midnight blue. The red is derived from madder root (Manjistha), black from fermented iron shavings and jaggery, and yellow from pomegranate rinds. Every pattern celebrates geometry and cosmic harmony.',
   53:     source: {
   54:       org: 'All India Handicrafts Board & Craft Council of India',
   55        citation: 'National Registry of Geographical Indications (GI Application #104)',
   56:       category: 'Traditional Sustainable Chemistry'
   57      },
   58:     followUps: [
   59:       'What makes Patan Patola double ikat so rare?',
   60:       'How does Jaipur Blue Pottery use glass without clay?',
   61        'Can I design my own block print in the Heritage Lab?'

   64    {
   65:     keywords: ['kolam', 'rangoli', 'mathematics', 'geometry', 'dots', 'pulli'],
   66:     response: 'Kolam is far more than decorative art—it is applied ethnomathematics! Computer scientists and mathematicians worldwide (including MIT and IMSc Chennai) have analyzed Kolams using Picture Languages and Array Grammars. Pulli Kolams (dot-grid designs) exhibit sophisticated topological properties: Eulerian paths (drawing a continuous closed loop visiting every node without lifting the hand), 4-fold and 8-fold dihedral symmetry, and recursive fractal scaling similar to Sierpinski carpets. Beyond mathematics, using coarse rice powder serves as Bhuta Yajna—feeding ants, beetles, and birds as the very first daily act of compassion.',
   67:     source: {
   68:       org: 'Indian Institute of Science (IISc) & IGNCA',
   69:       citation: 'Ethnomathematics in Sacred Folk Traditions of Tamil Nadu',
   70        category: 'Sacred Ethnomathematics'
   71      },
   72:     followUps: [
   73        'Open the Kolam Creator in Heritage Lab',
   74        'What is the meaning of Warli tribal painting?',
   75:       'How do Theyyam ritual dances work?'
   76      ]

   79      keywords: ['hornbill', 'nagaland', 'north east', 'tribes'],
   80:     response: 'The Hornbill Festival, held annually from December 1–10 at Kisama Heritage Village in Nagaland, is affectionately called the "Festival of Festivals". Named after the Great Indian Hornbill—a bird deeply venerated in Naga folk songs for its loyalty, alertness, and magnificent plumage—the festival unites all 17 major indigenous Naga tribes (including Angami, Ao, Konyak, Sumi, and Lotha). Each tribe showcases its unique Morung (bachelor dormitory architecture), war dances, indigenous archery, log-drum rhythms, and exquisite handwoven shawls with distinctive social heraldry.',
   81:     source: {
   82:       org: 'Department of Tourism & Art and Culture, Govt of Nagaland',
   83:       citation: 'Naga Cultural Heritage Compendium (Vol. 3)',
   84:       category: 'Indigenous Tribal Heritage'
   85      },
   86:     followUps: [
   87:       'Tell me about Majuli Island mask making in Assam',
   88:       'What is golden Muga silk?',
   89        'Explore North-East India on the Living Heritage Map'

   93      keywords: ['nataraja', 'shiva', 'dance', 'cern', 'cosmic'],
   94:     response: 'The Nataraja icon is a breathtaking synthesis of metaphysics and cosmic physics. Famed physicist Fritjof Capra wrote in *The Tao of Physics* that modern particle physics mirrors Shiva’s cosmic dance: dynamic patterns of energy continuously flowing between creation and annihilation. A life-sized bronze Nataraja statue stands at CERN (Geneva, European Organization for Nuclear Research) celebrating this profound parallel between subatomic dance and cosmic rhythm. Iconographically: the Damaru drum represents creation through sound; Agni represents thermodynamic entropy; Abhaya mudra promises refuge from fear; and Apasmara crushed beneath the foot represents victory over human ignorance.',
   95:     source: {
   96:       org: 'National Museum New Delhi & CERN Cultural Archives',
   97        citation: 'Chola Bronzes and the Metaphysics of Form (Ananda Coomaraswamy)',
   98:       category: 'Art History & Quantum Parallels'
   99      },
  100:     followUps: [
  101        'Inspect the Nataraja in 3D in Time Travel India',
  102:       'How is the lost-wax casting technique executed?',
  103        'What are the 9 Rasas in Bharatanatyam?'

  106    {
  107:     keywords: ['kalari', 'kalaripayattu', 'martial', 'kerala'],
  108:     response: 'Kalaripayattu, originating in Kerala during the Sangam and early medieval periods, is recognized as the mother of all Asian martial arts. Legendary monk Bodhidharma is said to have carried these breathing, flexibility, and empty-handed combat principles to China’s Shaolin Temple in the 5th century CE. Training takes place inside an excavated earth pit (Kalari) oriented east-west. It encompasses Meippayattu (body conditioning based on animal postures like lion, elephant, and serpent), Kolthari (wooden weapons), Angathari (metal blades and the lethal whip-like flexible sword Urumi), and Verumkai (barehanded pressure-point combat using Marma science).',
  109:     source: {
  110:       org: 'Kerala Kalamandalam & Ministry of AYUSH',
  111        citation: 'Traditional Martial and Marma Systems of Kerala (National Registry)',

  113      },
  114:     followUps: [
  115:       'What is Theyyam ritual in Malabar?',
  116:       'Tell me about the magic metal mirror Aranmula Kannadi',
  117:       'Take the South India Heritage Quiz'
  118      ]

  121  
  122: export function generateAruviResponse(query: string): AruviMessage {
  123:   const lower = query.toLowerCase().trim();
  124    
  125:   // Find match in curated database
  126:   const matched = curatedCulturalAnswers.find(item => 
  127:     item.keywords.some(kw => lower.includes(kw))
  128    );

  130    if (matched) {
  131:     return {
  132:       id: 'aruvi-' + Date.now(),
  133:       sender: 'aruvi',
  134        text: matched.response,
  135:       timestamp: 'Just now',
  136:       verifiedSource: matched.source,
  137:       audioAvailable: true,
  138:       suggestedFollowUps: matched.followUps
  139      };

  141  
  142:   // Culturally authentic dynamic synthesizer for any question
  143:   const responseText = `Thank you for asking about "${query}". In the vast tapestry of Indian heritage, this connects deeply with our civilizational emphasis on harmony between nature, sacred geometry, and community living. 
  144  
  145: Historical and anthropological records preserved by institutions like the Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) and Indira Gandhi National Centre for the Arts (IGNCA) emphasize that Indian traditions are never static relics—they are living systems (Jeevanta Parampara). Every regional expression, from temple architecture and handloom weaves to folk ballads and culinary wisdom, reflects centuries of empirical experimentation and sustainable community stewardship.
  146  
  147: Would you like to explore this further on our Living Heritage Map, inspect related artifacts in Time Travel India, or test your knowledge in Learn by Doing?`;
  148  
  149:   return {
  150:     id: 'aruvi-' + Date.now(),
  151:     sender: 'aruvi',
  152      text: responseText,
  153:     timestamp: 'Just now',
  154:     verifiedSource: {
  155:       org: 'Archaeological Survey of India & Sahitya Akademi',
  156        citation: 'Digital Heritage Knowledge Base (SIH 2026 Archive)',

  158      },
  159:     audioAvailable: true,
  160:     suggestedFollowUps: [
  161        'Show me Indian handloom traditions on the Map',

